(growth >= 2.6%) = 12.\nStep 2, C = P(core >= 2.7%) * P(growth < 2.0% | core >= 2.7%) = 0.27 * 0.68 (conditional lifted from 0.57 for the supply-constrained stagflation correlation) = 0.18 -> 18.\nStep 3, D = P(core < 2.0%) * P(growth < 1.7% | core < 2.0%) = 0.34 * 0.55 (conditional lifted from 0.42 for the demand-shortfall correlation) = 0.19 -> 19.\nStep 4, B = residual = 100 - 12 - 18 - 19 = 51.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED. (i) My CQ3 P50 of 1.85% sits inside B's literal 1.7-2.5% band, and B is my modal cell at 51% — consistent, and B additionally absorbs the low-growth/moderate-inflation leftover cell the lock assigns to it, which is why it exceeds the sum of C and D. (ii) A at 12 equals P(growth >= 2.6%) read off my own CQ3 distribution exactly, so A cannot be inflated by the historical 87% base rate. (iii) C at 18 is strictly below the 0.27 core->=2.7% marginal and below CQ2's 0.52 for the 10-year >=2.5% test, as the lock's narrower back-half window and the added growth condition both require. (iv) C + D = 37, close to twice the 18-19 each, reflecting genuine two-sided inflation risk rather than a one-directional stagflation lean — which is the honest reading of a tug-of-war with an early inflationary phase and a late disinflationary phase. (v) A + B = 63 and C + D = 37; P(growth < 2.0%) = 0.57 is reconciled because much of that mass lands in B's leftover cell (growth 1.7-2.0% with core 2.0-2.7%), not in C or D.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.059Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE Index trailing 24-month average published above 110 at any month-end (after a 2025 close near 64 and a currently-normalized level), sustained for three consecutive month-ends.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"My CQ5 conjunct-A marginal of 0.33 was too low against the ~25-30% 1